## Audit Timeline (AT)

The append-only trail read back: what happened to a record, newest first, grouped by day, with anything after sign-off called out.

```hbs
<AuditTimeline
  @entries={{@model.trail}}
  @signedOffAt={{@model.signedAt}}
  @limit={{20}}
/>
```

Args: `@entries` (Audit Entry-shaped objects), `@signedOffAt`, `@limit`, `@emptyMessage`.

**It renders `subjectTitle`, not the linked subject's current name.** An entry records what the thing was called when the decision was made, which is exactly what the Audit Entry block's snapshot exists for — reading through the link would quietly rewrite history every time something is renamed.

**The changed-since-sign-off marker is the judgement this block adds.** A trail that treats every entry alike makes the reader scan dates to answer the only question an external auditor actually asks. Entries after sign-off get a rule down the side and a count at the top.

`@entries` is typed structurally rather than importing Audit Entry, because the timeline renders from inside cards that the entry module may itself reach — the same acyclic reasoning as Document Preview.

## Example
The linked example is `example/AuditTimelineExample/trail` — four entries over the FY26 H2 report (created, submitted, approved, then evidence attached the next morning) with sign-off set at the approval, so the after-sign-off marker is visible rather than described. The page also renders the empty state.

## Non-goals
Does not write entries — Audit and the closing commands append them. Does not filter or search the trail. Not a diff: it says what happened, not what changed field by field.
